    "question": "What is emulsification in cooking, and how do I use it to make sauces that don't break?",
    "summary": "Emulsification in cooking is the process of combining oil and water into a stable mixture using emulsifying agents such as egg yolk lecithin or mustard compounds. Understanding the underlying science — including fat ratios, temperature control, and emulsifier capacity — allows cooks to build sauces…",
